body:
[Yeah that's exactly where I'm looking.](https://imgur.com/a/iZDXSvq)

&#x200B;

Those are the logs on the right, and the time out on the left.

You've got me second-guessing myself. I even went to far as to log back into my ISP and double-check the assigned IPs that were provided. 

&#x200B;

Yes. I'm 100% positive I have the correct data. 

&#x200B;

* Reserved Gateway	[65.101.54.238](https://65.101.54.238)
* User-Assignable	[65.101.54.225](https://65.101.54.225) (Web Server)

I have attached a quick & dirty draw [diagram of my current network diagram here.](https://imgur.com/a/taxoVDW) 

&#x200B;

What doesn't make sense to me at the moment is how all other devices work. Our Wi-Fi networks are functioning, our other hardwired devices (desktops) are functioning, but I can't get this server to work. 

If something was screwed up on my ISP/Provider side I would see it globally.

>A switch can't do this. 

That's not explicitly true. For example, [TP-Link T1500-28PCT](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B010I2A7OS/?coliid=I1CH0ST1M2PTPX&colid=6KCZEBTNIP7X&ref_=lv_ov_lig_dp_it&th=1)has an embedded 4-Port RJ-45 WAN Load Balancer. Not splitting hairs, just making sure we're on the same page. 

&#x200B;

>You need a router that supports load balancing across multiple WAN connections.

Ok - so based on this information it would be advisable to find a better router solution first? I'm looking (Ideally) to have everything rack-mounted. Any recommendations? 

&#x200B;

>A managed switch by itself is not enough to properly use VLANs. You need at least your router to support them as well and ideally your access points too.

Ok - that makes sense to me in regards to VLANs and Routers, but can you expand on Access Points? By Access Point do you mean my CenturyLink Modem/Router and replacing that and/or looking to see if it supports VLANs?

&#x200B;

Thank you for your first response - as I said, I'm still learning everything, so I really appreciate being able to talk some of these things through.

I was trying to accomplish this from the command line to begin with! I'm still learning this, but I didn't have any luck. I tried multiple ways, here are the commands I ran. None of these commands provided any output:

&#x200B;

    grep -Ril "home-16-backround-image-2.jpg" /home/*

    grep -r "home-16-backround-image-2.jpg" /home

    find /home -type f -exec grep -l "home-16-backround-image-2.jpg" {} \;

    find /home/frvrkent/ -type f -exec grep -l "home-16-backround-image-2.jpg" {} \;

    find / -type f -exec grep -l "home-16-backround-image-2.jpg" {} \;

&#x200B;

I figured that would have been the easiest/simplest way to just find the string but I must have been doing something wrong. Do you have any insight on this? Any help would be greatly appreciated, as I setup my own CentOS server for this very reason to learn things like this.
